# Rank 1123 / CVE-2022-36882: Jenkins git-plugin's notifyCommit webhook
# accepted an unauthenticated sha1 parameter and triggered polling for the
# exact commit named, with no verification that the caller was the git
# server it claimed to be, so anyone who could reach the endpoint could force
# a build of a chosen commit or drive branch/URL-confusion across jobs
# (CWE-352).
#
# GitStatus is an UnprotectedRootAction by design, so doNotifyCommit is meant
# to be reachable with no Jenkins session, the way a real git server's
# webhook call is. The vulnerable form took url, branches and sha1 straight
# from @QueryParameter and used them immediately, with nothing establishing
# that the caller had any relationship to the repository it named.
#
# Pool commit b46165c74a0b is the real fix (COMMIT_OK, SECURITY-284 /
# SECURITY-907). It adds a whole new ApiTokenPropertyConfiguration class and
# requires a token @QueryParameter that must validate against it before
# doNotifyCommit does anything with url, branches or sha1, unless an
# administrator has explicitly disabled the check via the
# NOTIFY_COMMIT_ACCESS_CONTROL system property. No pool correction.
#
# ATTENTION, and not the same shape as the doTest*/doCheck* validation family
# already covered at rank 1121. Those are internal admin-configuration
# endpoints where the generic fix is @RequirePOST plus
# Jenkins.get().checkPermission(Jenkins.ADMINISTER); doNotifyCommit is
# deliberately unauthenticated by design (an UnprotectedRootAction meant for
# external webhook callers with no Jenkins session at all), so neither
# @RequirePOST nor a standard permission check would even be the right fix,
# and indeed the real fix is a bespoke per-plugin API-token scheme instead.
# code.JenkinsBuildScheduleMissingPermission (CWE_862) is the nearest by
# theme, an unguarded action that ends in triggering a build, but its
# condition and its one binding both require an observed
# Jenkins.get.getQueue.schedule call and a hasPermission/checkPermission gate
# neither of which appears anywhere in this fix; doNotifyCommit reaches an
# SCM trigger/poll path several calls deeper, and what it is missing is a
# caller-identity check that has no relationship to Jenkins's permission
# model at all.
#
# Diagnosis: scanning either revision reports the same one unrelated finding,
# an sha1-to-response-header taint path neither this CVE's mechanism nor its
# fix touches.
#
# No definition change attempted.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1123: notifyCommit uses sha1 with no caller-identity check"
  lang java
  file GitStatus.java
  reject_review code.JenkinsBuildScheduleMissingPermission
  code
  ```
  @Extension
  public class GitStatus implements UnprotectedRootAction {

      public HttpResponse doNotifyCommit(HttpServletRequest request, @QueryParameter(required=true) String url,
                                         @QueryParameter(required=false) String branches,
                                         @QueryParameter(required=false) String sha1) throws ServletException, IOException {
          lastURL = url;
          lastBranches = branches;
          if (StringUtils.isNotBlank(sha1) && !SHA1_PATTERN.matcher(sha1.trim()).matches()) {
              return HttpResponses.error(SC_BAD_REQUEST, "Illegal SHA1");
          }
          final List<ResponseContributor> contributors = new ArrayList<>();
          for (Listener listener : Jenkins.get().getExtensionList(Listener.class)) {
              contributors.addAll(listener.onNotifyCommit(request.getHeader("User-Agent"), new URIish(url), sha1, branches));
          }
          return HttpResponses.ok();
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1123: notifyCommit validates an api token before doing anything"
  lang java
  file GitStatus.java
  reject_review code.JenkinsBuildScheduleMissingPermission
  code
  ```
  @Extension
  public class GitStatus implements UnprotectedRootAction {

      static String NOTIFY_COMMIT_ACCESS_CONTROL =
              SystemProperties.getString(GitStatus.class.getName() + ".NOTIFY_COMMIT_ACCESS_CONTROL");

      public HttpResponse doNotifyCommit(HttpServletRequest request, @QueryParameter(required=true) String url,
                                         @QueryParameter() String branches, @QueryParameter() String sha1,
                                         @QueryParameter() String token) {
          if (!"disabled".equalsIgnoreCase(NOTIFY_COMMIT_ACCESS_CONTROL)
                  && !"disabled-for-polling".equalsIgnoreCase(NOTIFY_COMMIT_ACCESS_CONTROL)) {
              if (StringUtils.isEmpty(token)) {
                  return HttpResponses.errorWithoutStack(401, "An access token is required.");
              }
              if (!ApiTokenPropertyConfiguration.get().isValidApiToken(token)) {
                  return HttpResponses.errorWithoutStack(403, "Invalid access token");
              }
          }
          lastURL = url;
          lastBranches = branches;
          if (StringUtils.isNotBlank(sha1) && !SHA1_PATTERN.matcher(sha1.trim()).matches()) {
              return HttpResponses.error(SC_BAD_REQUEST, "Illegal SHA1");
          }
          final List<ResponseContributor> contributors = new ArrayList<>();
          for (Listener listener : Jenkins.get().getExtensionList(Listener.class)) {
              contributors.addAll(listener.onNotifyCommit(request.getHeader("User-Agent"), new URIish(url), sha1, branches));
          }
          return HttpResponses.ok();
      }
  }
  ```
